I can't see either of those as being a good reason to quit in and of
themselves, honestly.  Taking them in sequence:

1) "An overwhelming lack of interest."

Before I would stop what I was doing completely, I might try to figure
out _why_ people weren't interested.  This could actually come up with a
lot of answers, so you should be prepared to have a very open mind.  The
first place I'd start in your case is:

-- It's a graphical Windows binary in an open-source console world.

Graphics bad?  Not at all!  Binary-only bad?  Not true either.  But the
majority of roguelikes are open-source, text-based/console-based, and
can be run on non-Windows systems... so you might be unintentionally 
targeting to a super-small subset of roguelike players.  I don't know
anything about the demographics and computer type of roguelike players'
systems, but I can guess that the above three things are not accidental.

2) "People don't like what you're doing."

Fuck 'em.  There is no shortage of Negative Nancies in this world; there
will always be someone who chirps at you to say "UR DOIN IT RONG."  As long 
as there are enough people who _do_ like what you're doing, keep it up.  

If _nobody_ likes what you're doing, you'll find out rapidly enough; 
people will simply not play your game, or you'll hear a _lot_ of people
telling you "UR DOIN IT RONG."  The former is more of concern than the
latter (though the latter shouldn't be ignored, if you run into that
circumstance).  If you really end up with this problem, again, you
really want to sit back and review what you're doing with an honestly
open mind... and that includes allowing the possibility that, in fact, 
you are doing it wrong.  :)

In that case, you have to decide if you're willing to go with the flow
or not.  I originally targeted Spork for the "advanced" player, and
while I do see a lot of those sort playing on my server, I also see far
more people who are relatively new to the game and/or inexperienced.
This is sort of cool, to me, but it's also quite unexpected, so I'm
having to consider carefully whether I want to make other types of
changes as _well_ as rebalancing the lategame... and also be more
careful to make sure I don't screw new players as a result of any
changes*.

> (personally, if I wanted people to play my new roguelike, I'd set up a 
> public server so that the effort threshhold for trying it was really low,
> but I've harped on this in RGRD enough, I'm sure...)

This was, of course, the exact reason I released Spork the way I did; a
public server accessible via telnet is usable by all platforms I care
about.  (Really, I just don't care if you can't play on your phone.)

It bears repeating here -- though in your case, you don't have that
luxury, being graphical and Windows.  (Unless you're already using a
client/server architecture of some kind, that is.)  It might be also
stated as "the easier you make it for people to download and play your
game, the more people you're going to get trying it in the first place,
which is all you can ask for; beyond that, your game will have to stand
on its own."

